{"name":"Natural Order","hex":"#77b033","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"natural","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Existing in nature.","Normally associated with a particular person or thing; inherently related to the nature of a thing or creature."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A native inhabitant of a place, country, etc.","A note that is not or is no longer to be modified by an accidental."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/natural"},{"term":"order","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Arrangement, disposition, or sequence.","A position in an arrangement, disposition, or sequence."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To set in some sort of order.","To arrange, set in proper order."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/order"}],"entries":[{"title":"Natural order (philosophy)","blocks":[{"text":"In philosophy, the natural order is the moral source from which natural law seeks to derive its authority. Natural order encompasses the natural relations of beings to one another in the absence of law, which natural law attempts to reinforce. In contrast, divine law seeks authority from God, and positive law seeks authority from government."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Die_Leiter_des_Auf-_und_Abstiegs.jpg/330px-Die_Leiter_des_Auf-_und_Abstiegs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_order_(philosophy)","wikidata":"Q6980751"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Die_Leiter_des_Auf-_und_Abstiegs.jpg/330px-Die_Leiter_des_Auf-_und_Abstiegs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Die_Leiter_des_Auf-_und_Abstiegs.jpg/330px-Die_Leiter_des_Auf-_und_Abstiegs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The med